# JSON save backups

Applies to every site under `websites/` whose content is stored in JSON, and to every new site copied from the latest finished site. Shared code lives in `packages/json-backup`. MySQL sites are out of this rule.

## Live writes
- Admin and public saves of JSON under `data/` or `content/` go through `writeJsonFileSync` from `src/vendor/json-backup/writeJsonFileSync`. On the current CMS that call sits in `src/repositories/jsonFileStore.ts` `persist()`, and in any other repository that writes its own JSON files (for example one file per article).
- On an older CMS, each service that saves JSON calls `writeJsonFileSync` instead of `fs.writeFileSync`.
- A changed file keeps the previous bytes in `data-backups/<path>/{daily,weekly,monthly,quarterly}/`. A period that already has a snapshot is left alone.
- One-off import and migration scripts keep a plain write.

```ts
import { writeJsonFileSync } from '../vendor/json-backup/writeJsonFileSync.js';

writeJsonFileSync(filePath, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\n`);
```

Use a `.js` suffix on that import when the site compiles with NodeNext. Adjust the relative path for the file you are in.

## Keep it on every new site
- Do not remove `src/vendor/json-backup/`, the `json-backup:build` script, the `data-backups/` line in `.gitignore`, or `data-backups` in `deploy.json` `protect` when cleaning a copied site.
- Rebuild the vendor file with `npm run json-backup:build` after changing `packages/json-backup`. Do not edit the vendor file by hand.
- `data-backups/` stays out of git. Deploy must not delete it (`protect`).

Install steps are in `packages/json-backup/INSTALL.md`.
